### The baseline is ALREADY DIRTY, and none of it is this run's doing

Three pre-existing items, all timestamped **before** this group first opened the console at
13:27 NZDT (00:27 UTC), and none of them recorded in `SESSION-HANDOVER-2026-10-23.md` or in the
campaign queue before today:

1. **`show exception log` is NOT None.** One entry:
   `2026 Oct 22 23:49:18 local7.debug awplus corehandler : Process snmpd (PID:1841) signal 11,
   core dumped to /flash/snmpd-swmrvl_armv8-sin_x96056-20261019-27-1-1792712958-1841.tgz`
   — snmpd took a SIGSEGV at 12:49 NZDT. **The core file was deliberately left on flash**: it is
   the only evidence of the crash, and removing it is the Test Engineer's call, not the tester's.
   Flash has 14.1 GB available, so it is not in anyone's way.
2. **TWO unexpected reboots, not one** (the sentinel corrected this from this very sample, which
   holds both):
   - `2026-10-22 23:55:56  Unexpected  System reboot`  — 12:55 NZDT
   - `2026-10-22 23:53:17  Unexpected  System reboot`  — 12:53 NZDT
   - the last **Expected** entry before them is `2026-10-22 01:10:14 Expected CLI(user request)`,
     yesterday, which bounds both events to this morning.
   Two restarts two minutes apart read differently from one: a single restart after a segfault
   is crash-and-recover, two in two minutes is a repeated fault or a short boot-loop that then
   settled. `show system` agrees with the later one — uptime `0 days 00:33:15` at 00:29 UTC, so
   the unit booted at 23:55 UTC. It has been stable since.
3. **Pre-existing boot-time kernel errors** in that boot's banner, written down here so that no
   later sample in this campaign reads them as new:
   `kern.err tpm tpm0: A TPM error (256) occurred attempting the self test` and
   `kern.err boardinfo-eeprom-i2c 1-0050 / 1-0052: Failed to read eeprom data for board_index 0`.

**What the window itself contains.** `tools/tb grep tb277 "Oct 22 23:4[5-9]|Oct 22 23:5[0-6]"`
over the raw sample returns 40 lines; the informative ones come after the boot:

```
2026 Oct 22 23:56:20 user.notice awplus IMISH[1799]: [SCRIPT]snmp restart isis
2026 Oct 22 23:56:20 user.notice awplus IMISH[1861]: [SCRIPT]snmp restart isis
2026 Oct 22 23:56:20 user.notice awplus IMISH[2232]: [SCRIPT]snmp restart isis
```

`[SCRIPT]` IMISH lines are the ART pipeline's signature, and the process that segfaulted was
**snmpd**. So the crash and the reboots sit inside somebody's SNMP test run — a much more
ordinary explanation than a spontaneous boot-loop. Not investigated further: it is not this
case. One thing this capture cannot settle is whether the 23:53:17 reboot completed, because
only ONE kernel banner (23:55:56) falls in the window.

**Effect on this case: none, and the case is NOT confounded.** Every item pre-dates the group,
and the after-sample below is a **diff against this baseline**, so a new core or a new reboot
would still be caught. Reported to the sentinel as `NEEDS TEST ENGINEER:` — non-blocking, per
the unattended constraint.

## Group rig — built once here; cases 2–4 inherit it

The recipe is **T9032's authored precondition**, which is the gold source for this bench. Sent
with `tools/tb cfg tb277 dot1x1304 u2 115200` at 13:34:47–13:34:54 NZDT:

```
awplus#configure terminal
awplus(config)#radius-server host 127.0.0.1 key awplus-local-radius-server
awplus(config)#aaa authentication dot1x default group radius
awplus(config)#radius-server local
awplus(config-radsrv)#server enable
awplus(config-radsrv)#nas 127.0.0.1 key awplus-local-radius-server
awplus(config-radsrv)#user ckpeap password ckpeap123
awplus(config-radsrv)#user cktls password cktls123
awplus(config-radsrv)#exit
awplus(config)#exit
```

Every line was accepted; there is no `% ` line anywhere in the transcript.

**`radius-server host 127.0.0.1` is load-bearing, and the queue's short-form recipe omitted it.**
`aaa authentication dot1x default group radius` dials the RADIUS **client** configuration; with
no `radius-server host` there is nothing for `group radius` to reach, even though the local
server is listening on 127.0.0.1. Both keys must match. This was found by reading T9032's
authored precondition rather than the queue summary, and the queue has been corrected.

Verified immediately afterwards:

- `show radius` → `Server Host : 127.0.0.1`, Authentication Port 1812, Accounting Port 1813, and
  the server table row `127.0.0.1  1812  1813  Unknown  Unknown`. `Unknown` becomes `Alive`
  after the first exchange (`platforms/x960.md` §7E.4).
- `show radius local-server nas` → `Total NAS: 1`, `127.0.0.1`, Message Authenticator `optional`.
- `show radius local-server user` → `Total users: 2`: `ckpeap`, `cktls`.
- `show radius local-server statistics` → `Server status : Run (administrative status is
  enable)`; `Enabled methods : MAC EAP-MD5 EAP-TLS EAP-PEAP`;
  `Available methods : MAC EAP-MD5 EAP-TLS EAP-PEAP`; **`EAP trustpoints : local`**; every
  counter 0.

### Trustpoint `local` already existed — nothing was created, and nothing may be removed

The dispatch warned that `crypto pki trustpoint local` creates the object on entry
(`platforms/x960.md` §7E.8) and said to remove it in teardown. **Measured before anything was
touched, doing that would have destroyed pre-existing device state:**

```
awplus#show crypto pki trustpoint
Trustpoint "local"
   Type                 : Self-signed certificate authority (RSA + ECDSA)
   Root Certificate     : RSA:  C29CD492 253DF5BD F849819E F12866D8 792C90BE
                        : ECDSA: 906D4096 FB243C13 F727C758 7F41DF0D CBCF1FB5
   Local Server (RSA)   : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
   Local Server (ECDSA) : Enrolled with this trustpoint.
   Server Key           : RSA: server-default
                        : ECDSA: server-default-ecdsa
```

and `show crypto key mypubkey rsa` lists the RSA key pair `server-default` bound to it — **yet
`show running-config | include crypto` returns nothing at all.** PKI state on this platform lives
on flash, not in configuration, which is why no earlier campaign's config diff ever showed it.
This case therefore entered no PKI sub-mode and removed no trustpoint. It matters for this case
in its own right: EAP-PEAP needs a server certificate for its TLS tunnel, and `local` is where
that certificate comes from.

## Steps, as designed and as run

### STEP 0 — baseline with the feature OFF: port1.0.48 forwards with no authentication at all

**Expected:** with no 802.1X configured, the port forwards normally, so any later block is
attributable to the feature rather than to a dead cable.

```
$ tools/tb ping tb277 eth4 10.38.22.66 4
4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss, time 3058ms
rtt min/avg/max/mdev = 0.386/0.425/0.536/0.067 ms
```

and `show auth` read `802.1X Port-Based Authentication: Disabled` in the setup reads above.

