twice. The first run (`S4`) printed a complete and internally coherent result — 22 EAPOL frames,
`RESULT ... expected failure, got failure -- MATCH`, exit 0 — but the JSON file it wrote
alongside disagreed with its own stdout, recording `"verdict": "timeout"` and
`"eapol_frames": 2`. Those two cannot both describe the same process: in `wpasup.py` the verdict
and the decoded frame list are both final before the JSON is built, so nothing in the code can
produce a disagreement. **I could not explain it, so I did not grade on it.** The leg was re-run
unchanged as `S4b`, whose stdout and JSON agree with each other (22 frames, `"verdict":
"failure"`) and with S4's stdout. **S4b is the graded evidence.** The S4 JSON is left on the box
at `/tmp/dot1x1304/T44270-S4-peap-nouser.json` as the record of the inconsistency, and
`tools/README.md` now carries the warning to check `wpasup.py`'s JSON against its stdout. A
measurement I cannot account for is not a measurement.

This leg also produced the clearest server-side evidence in the case. **The local RADIUS server
is freeradius and the PEAP inner method is MSCHAPv2 — both MEASURED here, not assumed:**

```
2026 Oct 23 00:45:31 daemon.notice awplus radiusd[5055]: (40)   Login incorrect (mschap: FAILED: No NT-Password.  Cannot perform authentication): [cknosuchuser] (from client 127.0.0.1 port 0 via TLS tunnel)
2026 Oct 23 00:45:31 daemon.notice awplus radiusd[5055]: (41) Login incorrect (eap_peap: The users session was previously rejected: returning reject (again.)): [cknosuchuser] (from client 127.0.0.1 port 5048 cli 00-0d-b9-55-77-0f)
2026 Oct 23 00:45:33 user.notice awplus 802.1X[1094]: 802.1X Authentication failed (retry=0) for cknosuchuser@000d.b955.770f on port1.0.48
```

Note `via TLS tunnel` on the first line: the inner MSCHAPv2 exchange really did run inside the
PEAP tunnel, which is precisely what "EAP-PEAP" names. Worth one caveat for whoever reads this
next — the message `mschap: FAILED: No NT-Password. Cannot perform authentication` is the
server's wording for **an identity it has never heard of**, not for a password-format problem.
Read literally it is misleading.

